WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:13.822
stretto reads ahead of your agent.

2
00:00:14.372 --> 00:00:17.835
Here's what it reads, where its lookups come from, and what it saves.

3
00:00:19.635 --> 00:00:21.379
An agent works in turns.

4
00:00:21.879 --> 00:00:27.058
On each turn, a model reads the whole conversation, then picks one next step.

5
00:00:27.408 --> 00:00:29.472
It answers, or it calls a tool.

6
00:00:29.972 --> 00:00:33.492
Each turn costs seconds and thousands of tokens.

7
00:00:33.492 --> 00:00:35.557
A task can take ten or twenty.

8
00:00:37.157 --> 00:00:40.895
Look closely, and many turns are decided before they begin.

9
00:00:41.395 --> 00:00:43.864
This customer's record lists two orders.

10
00:00:44.164 --> 00:00:47.022
So the next two turns will read those two orders.

11
00:00:47.522 --> 00:00:49.582
The tools made that choice.

12
00:00:49.582 --> 00:00:51.588
The model spends a turn typing it out.

13
00:00:53.188 --> 00:00:58.027
stretto sits between the agent and its tools, as an MCP proxy.

14
00:00:58.477 --> 00:01:02.881
When a result names what comes next, stretto makes those reads at once.

15
00:01:03.281 --> 00:01:06.635
They ride back inside the result the agent asked for.

16
00:01:07.235 --> 00:01:09.515
So the agent skips those turns.

17
00:01:09.515 --> 00:01:11.477
No new tools, and the same prompt.

18
00:01:13.277 --> 00:01:15.217
A flow only reads.

19
00:01:15.217 --> 00:01:17.829
It calls no tool the server marks as a write.

20
00:01:18.379 --> 00:01:21.379
A lookup the agent doesn't use is a detour.

21
00:01:21.379 --> 00:01:24.220
It costs tokens, and changes no state.

22
00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:29.185
The lookups come from your agent's own recorded sessions.

23
00:01:29.585 --> 00:01:34.366
stretto counts which reads follow each call, and where each argument came from.

24
00:01:34.816 --> 00:01:39.355
The result is a flow: a small file you can read, review and diff.

25
00:01:39.805 --> 00:01:44.884
In replay, ten of an agent's own sessions give most of what all of them do.

26
00:01:46.584 --> 00:01:51.465
The console puts your servers, sessions, flows and jobs in one place.

27
00:01:51.965 --> 00:01:56.200
A session shows each call, the lookups the flow made after it, and why.

28
00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:00.884
A flow shows its graph, and what would change at another threshold.

29
00:02:02.684 --> 00:02:06.817
Each lookup weighs two costs: a turn saved, against a detour.

30
00:02:07.267 --> 00:02:12.029
stretto makes it when the chance it's used before the next write clears the line.

31
00:02:12.479 --> 00:02:17.229
Measured live on τ²-bench, that line sits at about thirty percent.

32
00:02:17.779 --> 00:02:19.519
No model is asked.

33
00:02:19.519 --> 00:02:20.915
The reach decider counts.

34
00:02:22.715 --> 00:02:27.016
Live, on 28 τ²-bench retail and airline tasks:

35
00:02:27.366 --> 00:02:32.120
Claude Sonnet 5 took 20.5% fewer LLM turns.

36
00:02:32.670 --> 00:02:41.937
Claude Haiku 4.5 took 22.4% fewer, and GLM-5.3 27.9% fewer.

37
00:02:44.537 --> 00:02:52.162
Asking the agent to batch its calls saved 3.4 to 7.6%, not all of it established.

38
00:02:52.612 --> 00:02:55.692
A prompt batches what the agent already knows.

39
00:02:55.692 --> 00:02:57.945
A flow reads what results reveal.

40
00:02:58.395 --> 00:03:06.464
With the prompt in both arms, a flow still saved the Claude models 17.2 and 22.9%.

41
00:03:08.464 --> 00:03:13.193
stretto is open source, written in Rust, under the MIT license.

42
00:03:13.593 --> 00:03:17.035
Run stretto init in front of any MCP server.

43
00:03:17.435 --> 00:03:21.596
It learns from your agent's own sessions, with no key and no model.

44
00:03:23.896 --> 00:03:24.716
stretto.

45
00:03:24.716 --> 00:03:25.949
Read ahead of your agent.
